WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:11.440
In the last movie we talked about data science
without actually having Big Data.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:14.040
In this movie, we’ll take the compliment
of that.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:18.521
We’ll look at scenarios in which a person
works with Big Data, but doesn’t require

00:00:18.521 --> 00:00:21.450
the full data science skill set.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:26.970
As a way of reminding ourselves, Big Data
usually involves unusual volume, and velocity,

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:33.370
and variety in the data.

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:35.750
Data Science also has this little diagram.

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:40.030
It involves statistical skills, and domain
knowledge, and coding ability.

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:45.340
And the three of them together gets data science.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:50.800
Now let’s take a look at, can you do Big
Data with just two of the data science skills?

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:54.079
How about with just statistics and with coding?

00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:56.850
The answer of course is yes.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:00.420
Because that’s where we have machine learning.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.531
Machine learning is a very important area
of data science and this is where a computer

00:01:04.531 --> 00:01:08.710
program learns to adapt to new information
as it comes in.

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:13.900
The two most familiar examples are spam filters,
where the computer program learns that a particular

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:18.799
kind of email is spam or not, based on your
own individual responses, and based on the

00:01:18.799 --> 00:01:23.060
responses of millions of other people who
use the same email program.

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:28.200
Or, facial recognition and photographs, where
your computer program learns what face belongs

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:29.799
to whom.

00:01:29.799 --> 00:01:33.780
Here is an article from Nature that talks
about artificial intelligence and machine

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:35.780
learning in general.

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:40.829
If we scroll down a little bit, it talks specifically
about the problem of facial recognition and

00:01:40.829 --> 00:01:44.270
how the computers can learn to identify face.

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:49.480
It is something that is very easy for humans
to do, but much harder for a computer.

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:55.070
So machine learning is a good example of working
with Big Data because it can have volume,

00:01:55.070 --> 00:02:01.200
velocity, and variety, but you don’t necessarily
need to have domain knowledge, because the

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.219
computer is working without any knowledge
whatsoever.

00:02:05.219 --> 00:02:10.310
Another possibility is what Drew Conway, who
created this Venn Diagram, originally called

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:16.130
the Data Zone, that’s the combination of
coding, and domain knowledge without statistics.

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:20.110
Now he says, “danger” here, but there
are some very good examples of data science

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:23.600
here that do not involve statistical knowledge.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:28.430
The most common of which are word counts and
just parsing of natural language.

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:33.710
The most common tool used for this is what’s
called, the natural language tool kit.

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:38.170
This is a package that’s used in Python,
the programming language.

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:41.890
And it will ask people to do all sorts of
amazing things.

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:44.450
You can do things like word counts.

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:49.030
The best known word count was the one that
was used actually a few decades ago to identify

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:53.780
authorship of the Federalist letters in American
historical political science, all the way

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:59.150
up to the things like, comparing vocabulary
sizes of various Hip-Hop singers.

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:03.400
And so, there’s amazing things that can
be done with natural language by simply counting

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:07.881
how often the words occur, without requiring
any statistical knowledge per se, because

00:03:07.881 --> 00:03:10.980
there’s no influential procedure that goes
into it.

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:15.840
And now those are two of the combinations
of data science skills that we have two at

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.970
a time without Big Data.

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:24.820
That leaves a third one here, and that’s
statistics and substantive or domain expertise.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:27.120
And this is traditional research.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:31.980
Unfortunately, without the coding skills,
you’re not going to be able to deal with

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:36.790
the volume, the velocity, or the variety of
data that characterizes Big Data.

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:46.450
Tremendous things are accomplished in traditional
research, but Big Data is not one of them.

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:51.360
In summary, as far as you have at least two
of the items in the Venn diagram, you can

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:53.500
work with big data.

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:57.980
You can not work with Big Data if you have
just statistical knowledge, or just coding

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:00.160
skills, or just domain knowledge.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:04.510
Instead, what you would have to do in that
situation is collaborate with people who have

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:06.480
that information and skills.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:11.630
In fact, collaboration really is the rule
in data science, Because there is such a broad

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:16.410
range of skills that are necessary, nobody
is usually able to bring all of to it, but

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:18.620
they have to work together.

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:22.430
That actually is one of the wonderful things
because I think that most of the interesting

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:25.569
developments come about through collaboration.

00:04:25.569 --> 00:04:28.560
And that is something that data science encourages
strongly.

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:34.240
And so, when you look at the relationship
between data science and Big Data, the situation

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:36.159
isn’t exactly even.

00:04:36.159 --> 00:04:40.729
It’s possible to do data science with an
incomplete version of Big Data, but it’s

00:04:40.729 --> 00:04:45.209
much more difficult to do Big Data work without
the triumvirate of data science skills.

